WB.L("mona-lisa-theft", "arts", "beginner", "The day the Mona Lisa vanished",
  "In 1911 a handyman walked out of the Louvre with the world's most famous painting under his smock. Strangely, the theft is a big part of why it became so famous.",
  [
    ["A quiet Monday", "The Mona Lisa was stolen on Monday 21 August 1911, when the Louvre was closed for cleaning and workmen moved freely.",
      "Mondays were cleaning days at the Louvre. The galleries were shut and workmen wandered the halls. On the morning of 21 August 1911, a man in a white workman's smock lifted Leonardo da Vinci's portrait off its hooks in the Salon Carré. The painting is small, about 77 by 53 centimetres, and painted on a thin poplar panel rather than canvas. The thief carried it to a service stairwell, removed the heavy frame and protective glass box, and slipped the panel under his clothing. Accounts differ on whether he hid in a storeroom overnight or arrived that morning among the staff. Either way, he walked out into the Paris streets and nobody stopped him."],
    ["Nobody noticed", "The empty wall went unremarked for more than a day because paintings were often taken away for photography, and the search then chased the wrong suspects.",
      "The next morning, the painter Louis Béroud arrived to sketch and found four iron pegs where the Mona Lisa should have been. Guards assumed the museum's photographers had taken it, which happened regularly. Only when Béroud pressed them did the truth sink in. The Louvre closed for a week and the director lost his job.\n\nPolice followed strange trails. The poet Guillaume Apollinaire was jailed for several days because an acquaintance had earlier stolen ancient statuettes from the Louvre that Picasso had ended up with. Picasso was questioned too. Both were cleared. The real thief had been interviewed early, at his lodgings, and aroused no suspicion."],
    ["The inside man", "The thief was Vincenzo Peruggia, a craftsman who knew the Louvre and kept the painting in a trunk in his Paris room for more than two years.",
      "Vincenzo Peruggia was an Italian craftsman who had worked at the Louvre and helped install the very glass cases meant to protect its paintings. He knew the building, and the staff knew him. For more than two years he kept the Mona Lisa in a trunk with a false bottom in his Paris room.\n\nIn late 1913 he wrote to Alfredo Geri, an art dealer in Florence, offering to return the painting to Italy. Geri arranged a hotel meeting with Giovanni Poggi, director of the Uffizi Gallery. After examining the panel and checking the Louvre's markings on its back, they alerted police, and Peruggia was arrested."],
    ["Patriot or opportunist?", "Peruggia claimed patriotism, but his belief that Napoleon had looted the painting was wrong, and he had also asked for money.",
      "At his trial in Florence, Peruggia argued that he had acted out of patriotism. He believed, wrongly, that Napoleon had looted the painting from Italy. In fact Leonardo took it to France himself, and it entered the French royal collection long before Napoleon was born.\n\nPeruggia's story struck a chord with many Italians, and he received a fairly light sentence of a little over a year, of which he served only about seven months. Historians note that he had also asked for money, so his motives were probably mixed. Before going home, the painting was displayed in Florence, Rome and Milan to enormous crowds. It returned to the Louvre in early 1914."],
    ["How theft made a star", "The theft turned a respected masterpiece into the most famous painting in the world.",
      "Before 1911, the Mona Lisa was admired by art lovers, but it was not obviously the most famous painting on Earth. The theft changed that. For two years the story ran in newspapers worldwide, and photographs of the portrait were printed again and again. Visitors even queued at the Louvre to stare at the empty space on the wall. When the painting came back, it returned as a celebrity.\n\nArt historians often call the theft a turning point, alongside Leonardo's reputation and the picture's remarkable technique, such as the soft, smoky blending called sfumato. Today it sits behind protective glass and is seen by millions of visitors every year."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why could the thief move so freely through the Louvre?", ["It was a public holiday and the guards were away","The museum was being emptied for a big move","The galleries were closed and workmen were about","He had been handed a master key by the director"], 2, "Cleaning day meant closed galleries and workmen in smocks, so a man in a smock drew no attention."],
    [1, "Why did guards not raise the alarm straight away?", ["They assumed the photographers had taken it away","They believed it had been sent for restoration","They thought it had been lent to another museum","They had been told it was being sold abroad"], 0, "Paintings were routinely moved for photography, so an empty wall looked normal for more than a day."],
    [2, "How did Peruggia's insider knowledge help him?", ["He had designed the Louvre's alarm system","He had secretly drawn up the Louvre's floor plans","He had studied under Leonardo's descendants","He had worked there and the staff knew him"], 3, "Familiarity let him blend in and avoid suspicion, even when police interviewed him early on."],
    [3, "What was wrong with Peruggia's claim that he was returning the painting to Italy?", ["It had been painted in Paris to begin with","Leonardo took it to France himself, long before Napoleon","It had been given to France by Italy's king","Napoleon had bought it legally from its Italian owner"], 1, "Napoleon never looted it; it entered the French royal collection centuries earlier, and Peruggia had also asked for money."],
    [4, "How did the theft affect the painting's reputation?", ["It was proven a forgery and fell from favour","It lost value after being damaged in the theft","Two years of press coverage made it a celebrity","It stayed just as little known as it had been before"], 2, "Constant press coverage turned a respected masterpiece into the world's most famous painting."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A thief walked out of the Louvre with the Mona Lisa. The theft made it a star."});

WB.L("bayeux-tapestry", "arts", "beginner", "The Bayeux Tapestry: 1066 in wool",
  "A strip of linen nearly 70 metres long tells the story of the Norman conquest of England like a medieval comic strip. And technically, it isn't even a tapestry.",
  [
    ["Not a tapestry", "The Bayeux Tapestry is actually an embroidery: coloured wool stitched onto linen, not woven on a loom.",
      "A true tapestry has its picture woven into the cloth on a loom. The Bayeux Tapestry was made differently. Its images were stitched with coloured woollen yarn onto a long band of plain linen, which makes it an embroidery. It is roughly 68 metres long but only about half a metre tall, made from several pieces of linen joined together. The embroiderers used a handful of colours, mainly reds, yellows, greens and blues, yet created hundreds of people, horses, ships, buildings and animals. Short Latin captions run above the scenes. Read from left to right, it unfolds like a film reel, which is why it is often called the world's oldest comic strip."],
    ["Made by the conquered", "It was probably commissioned by Bishop Odo, William the Conqueror's half-brother, and stitched by English embroiderers in the 1070s.",
      "No document records who ordered it, but most historians think it was Odo, Bishop of Bayeux and half-brother of William the Conqueror. Odo appears prominently in the scenes, and Bayeux Cathedral was consecrated in 1077, a likely occasion for such a gift.\n\nStrangely for a French treasure, it was probably made in England. The style of the drawings and some of the spelling in the captions suggest English craftspeople, possibly in Canterbury, which was famous for its needlework. So the story of England's conquest may have been stitched by the conquered themselves, working for one of the new Norman rulers."],
    ["The oath of Harold", "The story is told from the winners' side: Harold swears an oath to William, so the Normans can brand him an oath breaker.",
      "The embroidery opens with the old king, Edward the Confessor, and his powerful nobleman Harold Godwinson. Harold sails to France, is captured, and ends up at the court of William, Duke of Normandy. He even joins William on a campaign in Brittany. Then comes the key scene: Harold swears an oath with his hands on holy relics.\n\nThe embroidery does not spell out the promise, but Norman sources claimed he swore to support William's claim to the English throne. When Edward died in January 1066 and Harold took the crown himself, the Normans could present him as an oath breaker. The tapestry tells the story from the winners' point of view."],
    ["Comet and invasion", "The embroidery shows Halley's Comet as an omen, then the everyday logistics of building and launching an invasion fleet.",
      "Soon after Harold is crowned, people point up at a star with flaming tails. This is Halley's Comet, which really did appear in the spring of 1066 and was widely seen as an omen.\n\nThe embroidery then shows something history books often skip: the logistics of invasion. Trees are felled, ships are built, and men carry armour, swords, spears and barrels of wine down to the boats. Horses are loaded and the fleet crosses the Channel. Once landed, the Normans build a fort and cook a feast. These everyday scenes are priceless to historians, offering rare pictures of eleventh century clothing, weapons, tools, ships and food."],
    ["Hastings and survival", "The tapestry ends with the Battle of Hastings in 1066, and has survived nine centuries of fires, revolutions and wars.",
      "The battle scenes are crowded and violent. Norman cavalry charge English soldiers in a shield wall on a hill. At one point William lifts his helmet to show he is still alive. Then comes Harold's death, though historians still debate which figure is Harold, and some think the arrow in the eye was altered in nineteenth century restoration. The final section is missing.\n\nThe first written record is an inventory of 1476, when it hung in Bayeux Cathedral. During the French Revolution it was nearly cut up to cover wagons, until a local official saved it. Napoleon displayed it while planning his own invasion of England. In 1944 it was moved to the Louvre for safekeeping, and today it is kept in Bayeux under low light."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is the Bayeux Tapestry technically not a tapestry?", ["It is painted in dye on a plain linen sheet","It is embroidered with wool on linen, not woven","It is printed onto silk using wooden blocks","It is too long to count as a single work"], 1, "A true tapestry is woven into the cloth, while this work is stitched onto it."],
    [1, "Where was the embroidery most likely made?", ["In Normandy, by monks in Bayeux Cathedral","In Rome, by craftspeople serving the Pope","In Flanders, by weavers working for Harold","In England, probably by English needleworkers"], 3, "The drawing style and the spelling in the captions suggest English craftspeople, perhaps in Canterbury."],
    [2, "Why does Harold's oath matter to the story as told?", ["It lets the Normans call Harold an oath breaker","It proves Harold had been crowned in Normandy","It shows William promised England to Harold","It explains why Edward left England in 1066"], 0, "The embroidery presents events from the winners' viewpoint, with Harold's broken promise as the justification for invasion."],
    [3, "Why do historians value the scenes of building ships and loading supplies?", ["They prove the invasion fleet was never delayed","They show which battles William won in France","They offer rare views of eleventh century daily life","They record the names of every Norman soldier"], 2, "These everyday scenes show how an invasion was really organised and what ordinary things looked like."],
    [4, "What do historians still debate about the death scene?", ["Whether Harold was ever actually crowned king in London","Which figure is Harold, and whether the arrow was altered","Whether a comet is shown in the battle scene itself","Whether William took any part in the battle at all"], 1, "Even the most famous scene is uncertain, partly because of nineteenth century restoration work."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"It is called a tapestry, but it isn't one. And the conquered may have made it."});

WB.L("birth-of-cinema", "arts", "beginner", "How the movies began",
  "A question about a galloping horse, a peephole machine and a café screening in Paris led to the most popular art form of the twentieth century. And one of the first feature films was Australian.",
  [
    ["The galloping horse", "In 1878 Eadweard Muybridge photographed a running horse in sequence, showing that all four hooves leave the ground, tucked under the body.",
      "For centuries, painters showed galloping horses with legs stretched out front and back like a rocking horse. Nobody could see fast enough to know. In California, the businessman Leland Stanford hired the photographer Eadweard Muybridge to find out whether all four hooves ever left the ground at once. In 1878, Muybridge set up a row of cameras along a track, triggered one after another as the horse raced past. The pictures showed that the hooves did leave the ground, but when they were tucked under the body, not stretched out. Muybridge then put his sequences on a spinning disc and projected them, so audiences watched the horse appear to run. Moving pictures had begun."],
    ["The illusion of motion", "Film is a row of still photographs shown fast enough that the brain's motion detection blends them into smooth movement.",
      "A film strip is a long row of photographs, each slightly different from the last. Shown quickly enough, the brain stops seeing separate pictures and perceives smooth movement. This was long explained by persistence of vision, the idea that each image lingers on the retina. Most researchers now think that is incomplete. The illusion mainly comes from how the visual system detects motion, filling in movement between closely spaced images, an effect psychologists call apparent motion.\n\nEarly silent films ran at varied speeds, often around 16 to 20 frames per second. When sound arrived in the late 1920s, 24 frames per second became the standard, and it is still common today."],
    ["Peephole versus screen", "Edison sold films for one viewer at a time, while the Lumière brothers projected them to a paying crowd in Paris in 1895.",
      "Thomas Edison's laboratory, with much of the work done by his assistant William Kennedy Laurie Dickson, used flexible celluloid film with holes punched along the edges. Their Kinetoscope, shown to the public in 1894, was a wooden cabinet with a viewing hole. One customer at a time peered in at a film lasting less than a minute: a strongman flexing, dancers, a sneeze. Edison thought individual viewing would be more profitable, which let rivals take the lead in projection.\n\nIn France, Auguste and Louis Lumière built the Cinématographe, a light, hand-cranked machine that could film, print and project. On 28 December 1895, beneath the Grand Café in Paris, they showed short scenes from life to a paying audience. The tale of viewers fleeing their train film has little reliable evidence."],
    ["Stories on screen", "Filmmakers learned tricks and editing, and by 1906 Australia's The Story of the Kelly Gang had become what is widely seen as the first feature-length story film.",
      "Georges Méliès, a Paris stage magician, discovered that stopping the camera, changing something and restarting could make people vanish. His 1902 film A Trip to the Moon became one of early cinema's most famous images. In 1903, Edwin S. Porter's The Great Train Robbery told an action story across separate scenes, and audiences learned to accept cuts.\n\nIn 1906, a Melbourne team including the Tait family filmed the bushranger Ned Kelly's story. The Story of the Kelly Gang ran about an hour, far longer than almost anything before, and is widely considered the first feature-length narrative film. Most of it is lost, since early nitrate film was fragile and flammable. Fragments restored by the National Film and Sound Archive joined UNESCO's Memory of the World Register in 2007."],
    ["The talkies arrive", "Synchronised sound in the late 1920s transformed cinema within a few years, ending careers but opening new forms.",
      "Silent films were rarely silent. Cinemas hired pianists, organists or whole orchestras, and some used narrators or sound effects. But recording dialogue that matched the actors' lips was hard. In 1927 Warner Bros released The Jazz Singer, with synchronised songs and a few spoken lines. Audiences were thrilled, and within a few years most studios had switched to sound.\n\nThe change was brutal for some. Actors whose voices did not suit the microphone lost careers, cinema musicians lost jobs, and cameras were temporarily trapped in soundproof booths. Yet sound also opened new forms, such as the musical. In barely three decades, moving pictures had gone from scientific curiosity to global art."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Muybridge's photographs reveal about galloping horses?", ["The hooves never all leave the ground together","The legs stretch out front and back, as painters showed","All four hooves leave the ground, legs tucked under","Only the back hooves ever leave the ground together"], 2, "The sequence showed the hooves airborne with legs gathered beneath the horse, not stretched out like a rocking horse."],
    [1, "Why do still frames appear to move when shown quickly?", ["The brain fills in movement between close images","The images physically blend together on the screen","The eye holds on to every frame for a full second","The projector bends the light between each frame"], 0, "Apparent motion, not simple persistence of vision, mainly explains the illusion."],
    [2, "Why did Edison's Kinetoscope lose ground to the Lumière brothers' machine?", ["It produced images too dim to see clearly","It cost far more than any projector to run","It could not use flexible celluloid film","It served one viewer at a time, not a crowd"], 3, "Edison favoured individual viewing, which let rivals take the lead in projection for audiences."],
    [3, "Why is The Story of the Kelly Gang famous?", ["It was the first film to use recorded dialogue","It is seen as the first feature-length story film","It was the first film ever shown in a theatre","It was the first motion picture made in colour"], 1, "At about an hour long in 1906, it was far longer than earlier films."],
    [4, "What was the effect of sound arriving in the late 1920s?", ["Audiences preferred silent films and sound faded","Cinemas kept to silent films because sound cost too much","Most studios switched to sound within a few years","It changed cinema only slowly, over several decades"], 2, "Sound transformed the industry quickly, costing some actors and musicians their work while opening new forms."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A question about a galloping horse helped start cinema. One early feature was Australian."});

WB.L("frida-kahlo", "arts", "beginner", "Frida Kahlo: painting her own reality",
  "A teenage bus crash left her in pain for life. From her bed, with a mirror above her, Frida Kahlo began painting the face that would become one of the most recognised in art.",
  [
    ["A girl from Coyoacán", "Frida Kahlo was born in 1907 near Mexico City, survived polio, and planned to study medicine, not art.",
      "Magdalena Carmen Frida Kahlo was born on 6 July 1907 in Coyoacán, then a village on the edge of Mexico City. Her father was a photographer of German origin and her mother was Mexican. Later she often gave her birth year as 1910, the year the Mexican Revolution began, linking herself to the birth of modern Mexico.\n\nAt about six she caught polio, which left her right leg thinner than the left. Bright and rebellious, she was admitted in 1922 as one of a small number of girls at the elite National Preparatory School, planning to study medicine. Art was not yet her ambition."],
    ["The accident", "A 1925 bus crash left her with lifelong pain, and while recovering in bed she began painting self-portraits using a mirror.",
      "On 17 September 1925, the bus Frida was riding in collided with a streetcar in Mexico City. She was eighteen. A metal handrail pierced her body, and she fractured her spine, pelvis, collarbone, ribs and right leg. Doctors doubted she would survive. She did, but she spent months in bed and lived with pain for the rest of her life, undergoing many operations.\n\nDuring her recovery, her mother had a special easel made so she could paint lying down, and a mirror fitted above her bed. With her own reflection as the most available subject, she began painting self-portraits. Medicine gave way to art."],
    ["Frida and Diego", "Her stormy marriage to the muralist Diego Rivera shaped her life, and the couple moved among artists, writers and revolutionaries.",
      "In 1928 Frida showed her paintings to Diego Rivera, already Mexico's most famous painter and a leader of the mural movement, which covered public walls with scenes of Mexican history and workers' lives. He encouraged her, and they married in 1929. He was about twenty years older and much larger, and her parents reportedly compared the match to an elephant marrying a dove.\n\nBoth had many affairs, and Rivera's affair with Frida's sister Cristina hurt her deeply. They divorced in 1939 and remarried in 1940. They travelled to the United States, where Rivera painted murals, and their circle included artists, writers and political figures, among them the exiled Russian revolutionary Leon Trotsky."],
    ["Her own reality", "Kahlo painted her pain and identity in about 55 self-portraits, and rejected the Surrealist label because she painted her reality, not dreams.",
      "Kahlo made around 55 self-portraits, more than a third of her known paintings. She kept her joined eyebrows and faint moustache, and often wore traditional Tehuana dresses from southern Mexico, celebrating Indigenous culture. Her small, detailed works drew on folk art and devotional paintings called retablos, and were frank about pain, miscarriage, surgery and heartbreak. In The Two Fridas (1939), two versions of herself sit hand in hand, their exposed hearts joined by a single vein.\n\nThe Surrealist leader André Breton visited in 1938 and arranged a Paris exhibition in 1939. The Louvre bought her self-portrait The Frame, making her the first twentieth century Mexican artist in its collection. Yet she said she did not paint dreams but her own reality."],
    ["Final years and fame", "Kahlo attended her first Mexican solo show from her bed, died in 1954, and became a global icon decades later.",
      "Her health worsened in the 1940s and 1950s. She wore supportive corsets, some of which she painted on, and had several spinal operations. In 1953 part of her right leg was amputated because of gangrene. That year she had her first solo exhibition in Mexico. Told by doctors not to attend, she arrived by ambulance and received guests lying on her four poster bed, which was installed in the gallery. She died at the Blue House on 13 July 1954, aged 47.\n\nIn life she was often seen as Rivera's wife. From the 1970s feminist art historians, Mexican American artists and a 1983 biography broadened her audience. In 2021 Diego and I sold for about 35 million US dollars, then a record for a Latin American artist."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Kahlo originally plan to study?", ["Medicine","Painting","Photography","Architecture"], 0, "Art was not yet her ambition when she entered the National Preparatory School planning a medical career."],
    [1, "How did Kahlo begin painting self-portraits?", ["As a student, copying her father's photographs","While travelling in the United States with Rivera","While recovering in bed, using a mirror above her","As a teacher, using her students as models"], 2, "Her own reflection was the most available subject during months of recovery."],
    [2, "What was Diego Rivera's place in Kahlo's life?", ["A French critic who arranged her Paris show","A leading muralist, whom she married in 1929","Her art teacher at the National Preparatory School","A Russian revolutionary exiled in Mexico"], 1, "Rivera was already celebrated when she showed him her work, and their marriage was central to her life."],
    [3, "How did Kahlo respond to being called a Surrealist?", ["She rejected it: she painted her own reality","She embraced it and joined their Paris group","She said she had never heard of the movement","She agreed but only for her early works"], 0, "Her strange images, like roots growing from her body, expressed real experiences of pain and identity."],
    [4, "How did Kahlo attend her first solo exhibition in Mexico in 1953?", ["She sent a letter and stayed away on medical advice","She attended on crutches once her leg had healed","She let Rivera open it while she stayed home","She arrived by ambulance and greeted guests from her bed"], 3, "Against her doctors' advice, she arrived by ambulance and greeted guests lying on her four poster bed, which had been installed in the gallery."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A mirror above a sickbed changed the course of one woman's life and art."});

WB.L("sistine-chapel-ceiling", "arts", "beginner", "Michelangelo and the Sistine Chapel ceiling",
  "A sculptor who didn't want the job spent four years painting a chapel ceiling twenty metres above the floor. He didn't do it lying on his back, either.",
  [
    ["A reluctant painter", "Pope Julius II hired Michelangelo, who saw himself as a sculptor, to paint the Sistine Chapel ceiling in 1508.",
      "In 1508, Michelangelo Buonarroti was in his early thirties and already famous for his marble David in Florence and his Pietà in Rome. He thought of himself first as a sculptor. Pope Julius II, a forceful and warlike pope, had hired him to carve a grand tomb, but then turned to decorating the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, built by his uncle, Pope Sixtus IV. Michelangelo had little experience of fresco and accepted reluctantly. The walls already held frescoes by leading painters such as Botticelli and Perugino. The ceiling, painted as a starry sky, was about to be transformed."],
    ["Painting on wet plaster", "Fresco means painting on wet plaster, so the artist must finish each section before it dries.",
      "Fresco means fresh in Italian. The painter applies pigments mixed with water onto wet lime plaster. As the plaster dries, a chemical reaction locks the colour into the wall, making it extremely durable. The catch is speed. Each day only as much plaster as could be painted before it set was laid. These daily patches are called giornate, from the Italian for a day's work. Mistakes could not be painted over; the plaster had to be chipped away and redone. Michelangelo's team transferred his designs to the plaster using full-size drawings called cartoons, either by pricking holes along the lines or by pressing them into the soft surface."],
    ["Standing, not lying down", "Michelangelo painted standing on a scaffold with his head tilted back, helped by assistants, not lying flat and alone.",
      "A popular image shows Michelangelo lying on his back for years, paint dripping onto his face. In reality, he designed a wooden scaffold that bridged the chapel just below the ceiling, leaving the floor free for services. He and his assistants worked standing, heads tilted back and arms raised.\n\nIt was still punishing. Michelangelo wrote a comic poem, with a little sketch in the margin, complaining about his aching neck, bent back and the paint dripping onto his face. Nor did he work entirely alone, as another myth claims. Assistants ground pigments, prepared plaster and helped with some painting, although he painted the great majority of the figures himself."],
    ["The story overhead", "The ceiling tells nine Genesis scenes, including the famous Creation of Adam, though the hidden brain theory has no evidence behind it.",
      "The ceiling is about 40 metres long and 13 metres wide, with more than 300 figures. Down the centre run nine scenes from Genesis, from God separating light from darkness to the story of Noah. Around them sit huge prophets and sibyls, prophetesses from the classical world, and muscular nude youths called ignudi on painted architecture that only looks three-dimensional. Michelangelo painted the scenes in reverse story order, starting near the entrance with Noah, and his figures grew larger as he went.\n\nIn The Creation of Adam, fingertips almost meet. Some writers say the shape around God resembles a brain, hidden by Michelangelo the anatomist, but there is no evidence he intended it."],
    ["Judgment and restoration", "Michelangelo returned to paint The Last Judgment, and a 1980 to 1994 restoration revealed that his colours were bright, not dark.",
      "The ceiling was unveiled in 1512 and stunned everyone who saw it. More than two decades later, Michelangelo painted The Last Judgment across the wall behind the altar, working from 1536 to 1541. Christ raises the saved while the damned tumble towards hell. Some church officials were scandalised by the nudity, and after his death Daniele da Volterra was hired to paint drapery over figures, earning the nickname Il Braghettone, the breeches maker.\n\nFor centuries, candle and incense smoke and old varnish darkened the ceiling. A Vatican restoration from 1980 to 1994 revealed vivid pinks, greens, oranges and blues. It was controversial, with some critics arguing that final touches were lost. The chapel still hosts the conclave that elects a pope."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Michelangelo see himself when he accepted the commission?", ["As an architect with years of fresco work","First of all as a sculptor, new to fresco","As a poet who painted only now and then","As a fresco master picked for his skill"], 1, "He accepted the ceiling reluctantly because sculpture, not painting, was his main art."],
    [1, "Why did fresco painters work in daily sections called giornate?", ["Each section needed a different pope's approval","Pigments faded if they were mixed days earlier","The scaffold could reach only a small area at once","Paint must go on before each patch of plaster dries"], 3, "Colour sets into wet plaster as it dries, so each day's patch had to be finished before it hardened."],
    [2, "How did Michelangelo actually paint the ceiling?", ["Standing on a scaffold, head tilted back","Lying flat on his back on a high platform","Hanging in a sling from the chapel roof","From the chapel floor using a long brush"], 0, "The lying-down image is a myth. He designed the scaffold himself, and the work was still punishing for his neck and back."],
    [3, "What is the status of the idea that a brain is hidden in The Creation of Adam?", ["Confirmed by his own written notes","Confirmed by Vatican experts after the restoration","A modern idea with no evidence he intended it","Described in his poem about the ceiling"], 2, "Michelangelo had studied anatomy, but there is no evidence he hid a brain deliberately."],
    [4, "What did the 1980 to 1994 restoration reveal?", ["Bright colours hidden under centuries of grime","That the ceiling had first been painted gold","That most figures were painted by assistants","That the figures had been painted on canvas"], 0, "Candle smoke and old varnish had made the colours look sombre, so vivid pinks and blues surprised everyone."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Michelangelo did not paint the Sistine ceiling the way the legend says."});

WB.L("stradivarius-violins", "arts", "intermediate", "The Stradivarius mystery",
  "Violins made in a small Italian town three centuries ago sell for millions of dollars. But when top players are blindfolded, can they actually tell the difference?",
  [
    ["The master of Cremona", "Antonio Stradivari worked in Cremona into his early nineties, and his instruments from about 1700 to 1720 are called his golden period.",
      "Cremona, in northern Italy, was the heart of violin making for generations. The Amati family established the craft there in the 1500s, and the Guarneri family also worked in town. Antonio Stradivari was born around 1644 and may have trained with Nicolò Amati. He worked for an extraordinary length of time, making violins, violas, cellos, guitars and harps until he died in 1737, in his early nineties. Estimates suggest he made a thousand or more instruments, and roughly 650 are thought to survive, though the numbers are approximate. Experts generally regard instruments from about 1700 to 1720 as his golden period, when his designs were most refined."],
    ["Searching for the secret", "A violin's sound comes from its whole wooden body, and theories about Stradivari's secret, from cold-climate wood to chemicals, remain unproven.",
      "A string alone makes little sound. Its vibrations pass through the bridge into the body, a thin spruce top and maple back, which amplifies and shapes the tone. Tiny differences in wood density, thickness and varnish change which frequencies are boosted.\n\nPeople have long hunted for a lost secret. One theory points to the Maunder Minimum, a period of low solar activity in the Little Ice Age, when slow-growing trees may have produced unusually dense wood. Other research suggests the wood was treated with minerals or chemicals, perhaps against woodworm. None is proven, and many experts think Stradivari's skill matters more than any single ingredient."],
    ["The blindfold tests", "In controlled blind tests, experienced violinists could not reliably tell Stradivari violins from good new ones.",
      "From 2010, the French acoustics researcher Claudia Fritz and American violin maker Joseph Curtin ran careful experiments. In the first, published in 2012, experienced violinists wore modified welder's goggles so they could not see which instrument they were playing. They tried violins by Stradivari and Guarneri alongside high quality new ones. Players could not reliably tell old from new, and the most preferred instrument was a new one. A larger 2014 follow-up with leading soloists found similar results, and later concert hall tests found listeners often thought the new violins projected better. These studies do not prove old violins are poor, only that their superiority is far less obvious than legend suggests."],
    ["Why the legend endures", "Old Italian violins keep their value through rarity, history and the effect of belief, not sound alone.",
      "If blindfolded players struggle to pick them, why do Stradivari violins stay so valuable? They are rare historical objects, like Old Master paintings, and their price reflects scarcity, craftsmanship and provenance as much as sound. Many have names and long histories, passing through the hands of famous musicians.\n\nSome violinists argue that lab tests cannot capture what it feels like to live with an instrument for years, learning its responses in large halls. Belief may play a part too. Knowing you are playing a three hundred year old masterpiece can change how you play and how listeners hear. Researchers still debate how much of the magic is acoustic and how much is psychological."],
    ["Million-dollar instruments", "Top Stradivari violins sell for millions, so most are owned by foundations and loaned to players.",
      "In 2011, the Stradivari known as the Lady Blunt sold for about 15.9 million US dollars, with the proceeds going to relief efforts after the earthquake and tsunami in Japan. Few working musicians could ever afford such prices, so many of the finest instruments are owned by banks, foundations, governments or wealthy collectors, who lend them to talented performers. In Australia, for example, some leading players perform on historic Italian instruments on loan.\n\nA few are kept almost unplayed in museums, such as the Messiah violin at the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, prized partly because it looks nearly new. Most are still played, which many musicians feel is what they were made for."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is unusual about Stradivari's working life?", ["He kept making instruments until his early nineties","He retired from instrument making in his forties","He made violins only and never any other instrument","He sold his workshop and moved abroad in old age"], 0, "He worked until he died in 1737, in his early nineties, making violins, violas, cellos, guitars and harps."],
    [1, "What does the 'Little Ice Age' theory propose?", ["Cold weather made the varnish harder and shinier","Slow-growing trees gave unusually dense wood","Frozen rivers let wood be shipped faster to Cremona","Cold winters gave the strings a brighter tone"], 1, "It is one unproven idea, and many experts think the maker's skill matters more."],
    [2, "What did the blind tests of Fritz and Curtin find?", ["Players could not reliably tell old from new","Players always picked the Stradivari correctly","Audiences preferred old violins in concert halls","Players could tell them apart only by touch"], 0, "Experienced violinists often preferred a new instrument, so the old violins' advantage is far less clear than legend suggests."],
    [3, "Why do Stradivari violins stay so valuable despite the tests?", ["Modern violins cannot be played in large halls","They are made of a wood that no longer exists","Rarity, history and belief add value beyond sound","The blind tests were later withdrawn as invalid"], 2, "Their price reflects scarcity, craftsmanship and provenance, and belief may change how they are played and heard."],
    [4, "Why are many top Stradivari instruments loaned to performers?", ["They sound best when passed between many players","Few players can afford multi-million-dollar prices","Owners are required by law to lend them out","Their makers' wills forbade private ownership"], 1, "Foundations, banks and collectors own many of them and lend them to talented players."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"When top violinists play blindfolded, can they pick the multi-million-dollar violin?"});

WB.L("rite-of-spring-premiere", "arts", "intermediate", "The night The Rite of Spring caused an uproar",
  "In Paris in 1913, a new ballet about an ancient sacrifice provoked jeers, shouting and scuffles in the audience. Within a year, its music was being cheered.",
  [
    ["Ballets Russes, pagan rites", "The Rite of Spring was a Ballets Russes work about ancient pagan rituals, ending with a young woman dancing herself to death.",
      "Before the First World War, Europe's most exciting dance company was the Ballets Russes, run by the Russian impresario Sergei Diaghilev. His star dancer was Vaslav Nijinsky, and his young composer was Igor Stravinsky, who had already scored hits in Paris with The Firebird (1910) and Petrushka (1911). With the painter Nicholas Roerich, Stravinsky devised a ballet about pagan rituals in prehistoric Russia, with Nijinsky as choreographer.\n\nThe Rite of Spring has no conventional plot. Tribes celebrate spring with dances, and a young woman is chosen for sacrifice. In the Sacrificial Dance she dances in a frenzy until she dies. It premiered on 29 May 1913 at the new Théâtre des Champs-Élysées in Paris."],
    ["Music like nothing before", "Stravinsky's score used a very high opening bassoon, harsh repeated chords and unpredictable rhythms to create something savage and new.",
      "The music opens with a bassoon playing a folk-like melody so high in its range that many listeners could not identify the instrument. Soon the orchestra builds layers of overlapping tunes. Then comes a famous passage where strings hammer the same harsh chord again and again, with accents falling in unpredictable places, so the listener cannot settle into a regular beat. Elsewhere the time signature changes almost every bar.\n\nStravinsky used a very large orchestra, with extra woodwinds, brass and percussion. Many melodies were based on or inspired by Russian folk tunes, but they were reshaped into something entirely new and startling."],
    ["Dancing against ballet", "Nijinsky's choreography rejected ballet's grace for stamping, hunched, inward-turned movements.",
      "Classical ballet prized elegance: turned-out feet, lifted bodies and the illusion of weightlessness. Nijinsky did the opposite. His dancers turned their toes inwards, hunched their shoulders, jumped heavily, landed with a thud and stamped in rhythmic groups. Roerich's costumes were heavy, colourful smocks inspired by old Russian peasant dress. To many in the audience it looked ugly or even ridiculous, and to others daringly modern.\n\nThe dancers found the shifting rhythms extremely hard to follow. Nijinsky's choreography was performed only a handful of times and then largely lost, until it was reconstructed for the Joffrey Ballet in 1987 from notes, drawings and memories."],
    ["Uproar in the theatre", "The premiere was interrupted by laughter, shouting and arguments, but the legend of a full-scale riot has probably grown in the retelling.",
      "Soon after the curtain rose, parts of the audience began to laugh, whistle and jeer, while others shouted back in defence of the work. The noise grew so loud that the dancers struggled to hear the orchestra, and Nijinsky stood on a chair in the wings shouting the counts to them. Stravinsky left his seat and went backstage.\n\nExactly what happened is hard to pin down. Many famous descriptions were written years or decades later and contradict each other. Some describe fist fights and objects being thrown, and stories circulated of police being called. Historians agree there was a real uproar, but caution that the legend of a full-scale riot has probably grown."],
    ["From scandal to classic", "Heard without the dancers in 1914, The Rite of Spring was a triumph and became a cornerstone of twentieth century music.",
      "The ballet was performed only a few more times in 1913. But in April 1914 the music was played as a concert piece in Paris without dancers, and this time it was a triumph, with Stravinsky reportedly carried from the hall on admirers' shoulders. Freed from the startling choreography, audiences could focus on the score's raw power.\n\nThe Rite became one of the most influential works of the century, shaping later classical music, jazz and film scores. In 1940, Walt Disney used it in the film Fantasia, set to scenes of the early Earth and dinosaurs. Today it is a regular part of orchestral programmes, and dozens of choreographers have created their own versions of the ballet."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happens in the ballet's final scene?", ["Rival tribes sign a peace and celebrate the harvest","A prince breaks a spell and marries a swan","An elder is crowned king of the spring tribes","A chosen young woman dances in a frenzy until she dies"], 3, "The ballet has no conventional plot; it ends with a sacrifice meant to bring back spring."],
    [1, "Why did the opening of the score puzzle listeners?", ["A loud fanfare from the whole brass section","A bassoon played so high few could name it","A choir singing in Latin with no orchestra","A long silence before any instrument played"], 1, "The unusual high register was one of many surprises in a score of harsh chords and shifting rhythms."],
    [2, "How did Nijinsky's choreography differ from classical ballet?", ["Heavy stamping, hunched shoulders, toes turned in","Graceful leaps and a floating, weightless style","Dancers who spoke and sang instead of moving","Exact copies of traditional Russian folk steps"], 0, "Instead of elegance and apparent weightlessness, he chose heavy, inward, primal movement."],
    [3, "What do historians say about the famous 'riot' at the premiere?", ["No disturbance, and the audience applauded","Police stopped the show and arrested the composer","A real uproar, but the legend has grown since","A full riot, confirmed by every account"], 2, "Accounts written years later contradict each other, so the exact violence is uncertain."],
    [4, "How was the music received when played as a concert piece in 1914?", ["It caused another uproar and was withdrawn","It was a triumph, without the startling choreography","It was ignored by critics and audiences","It was received politely but forgotten quickly"], 1, "Without the dance, audiences could focus on the score's power, and it became a classic."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A ballet premiere in 1913 caused an uproar. A year later, the same music was cheered."});

WB.L("bauhaus-design-school", "arts", "intermediate", "The Bauhaus: the school that designed the modern world",
  "A German art school lasted only fourteen years before the Nazis shut it down. Yet its ideas are in your kitchen chairs, your fonts and the buildings you walk past every day.",
  [
    ["Art meets craft", "Walter Gropius founded the Bauhaus in 1919 to unite art and craft, so students learned both principles and hands-on skills.",
      "In 1919, just after the First World War, the architect Walter Gropius founded a school in Weimar, Germany, by merging an art academy with a school of arts and crafts. He called it the Staatliches Bauhaus, roughly meaning state house of building. Gropius believed the old split between fine artists and craftspeople was harmful. Painters looked down on furniture makers, while industry churned out poorly designed products. At the Bauhaus, students learned artistic principles and hands-on skills in workshops for metal, wood, textiles, pottery, printing and more. The long-term goal was building, bringing all the arts together. Germany was poor and divided, and the school hoped to help shape a better society."],
    ["Learning to see again", "Every student began with a preliminary course that stripped away old habits and explored materials, shape and colour.",
      "New students first took the preliminary course, developed by Johannes Itten and later run by László Moholy-Nagy and Josef Albers. The aim was to unlearn old academic habits and experiment directly with materials. Students explored the textures of wood, glass, paper and metal, studied contrasts of light and dark, and built structures from folded paper. Albers later became famous for his studies of how colours change depending on their neighbours. The painters Paul Klee and Wassily Kandinsky taught theories of form and colour. It was one of the most remarkable teaching staffs ever gathered."],
    ["Objects and women", "Bauhaus workshops designed simple objects for industry, but women who were promised equality were steered towards weaving.",
      "The school shifted from handcraft towards designing prototypes for industry. Marianne Brandt designed teapots and lamps. Marcel Breuer designed a tubular steel armchair in the mid 1920s, later called the Wassily chair, inspired by the steel tubes of his bicycle. Herbert Bayer designed clean lower case typefaces. Many Bauhaus designs are still made today.\n\nThe school officially welcomed women, and in its first year more women than men applied. Leaders worried, and many women were pushed towards weaving. They turned that limitation into an achievement: under Gunta Stölzl, the first female master, the weaving workshop became one of the most commercially successful, and Anni Albers developed new textiles. Brandt broke into metalwork."],
    ["Dessau and the Nazis", "Pressure from conservative politicians, then the Nazis, forced the Bauhaus from Weimar to Dessau and Berlin, and closed it in 1933.",
      "Conservative politicians in Weimar saw the Bauhaus as foreign, left-wing and a waste of public money, and cut its funding. In 1925 it moved to Dessau, where Gropius designed an icon of modern architecture, completed in 1926: a glass curtain wall, flat roofs and linked wings. Gropius stepped down in 1928, followed by Hannes Meyer and, from 1930, Ludwig Mies van der Rohe. The Weimar and Dessau buildings are now UNESCO World Heritage sites.\n\nThe Nazis attacked it as un-German and communist. After they won control of Dessau's council in 1932, the school was forced out. Mies tried to continue in Berlin, but police raided it, and the staff closed it in 1933. Jewish and left-wing members suffered persecution, and some were killed in the Holocaust."],
    ["A worldwide legacy", "Bauhaus teachers spread its ideas across the world, though the phrase 'form follows function' came from Louis Sullivan.",
      "In fourteen years the school had perhaps 1,250 students, a small number for such influence. Its closure scattered its ideas worldwide. Gropius taught at Harvard, and Mies led the architecture school at what became the Illinois Institute of Technology, designing glass and steel towers that defined the modern skyline. Moholy-Nagy founded the New Bauhaus in Chicago in 1937. Josef and Anni Albers taught at Black Mountain College in North Carolina. Bauhaus-trained architects helped build the White City of Tel Aviv, now a World Heritage site.\n\nA common myth credits the Bauhaus with the slogan form follows function, but it came from the American architect Louis Sullivan in the 1890s. Still, it suits the Bauhaus well."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Gropius's central aim for the Bauhaus?", ["To train only architects for the German government","To teach painting as a pure art, apart from industry","To unite fine art and craft, working towards building","To keep painters and craftspeople in separate schools"], 2, "He thought the split between artists and craftspeople was harmful and wanted all the arts brought together."],
    [1, "What was the purpose of the preliminary course?", ["To unlearn old habits and explore materials","To teach students to copy old masters exactly","To choose which students would become teachers","To train students in business and printing"], 0, "It cleared away academic habits so students could experiment directly with materials."],
    [2, "How did the weaving workshop become important at the Bauhaus?", ["It was chosen as the school's only industrial workshop","Women steered into it made it a commercial success","It was led by Gropius, who preferred textiles","It replaced metalwork once funding ran out"], 1, "A limit placed on women became an achievement under Gunta Stölzl, the first female master."],
    [3, "Why did the Bauhaus close in 1933?", ["Its funding ended soon after the First World War","Its students all emigrated to the United States","Gropius sold the Dessau building to the council","Nazi pressure and a police raid led staff to close it"], 3, "Attacked as un-German and left-wing, it was forced out of Dessau in 1932 and closed in Berlin."],
    [4, "What is true of the slogan 'form follows function'?", ["It predates the Bauhaus but suits its ideas well","Gropius coined it in his 1919 manifesto","It was the school's official motto from 1919","Mies adopted it after leaving Germany"], 0, "It came from the American architect Louis Sullivan in the 1890s, so it is often wrongly credited to the Bauhaus, though it fits the school's ideas."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The Bauhaus lasted fourteen years, yet its ideas are probably within arm's reach of you."});

WB.L("how-music-was-written-down", "arts", "intermediate", "How music learned to be written down",
  "For most of history, music lived only in memory. The squiggles, lines and dots that now let anyone read a song were invented bit by bit over a thousand years.",
  [
    ["Music without paper", "For most of history, music was passed on by ear and memory, and early notation systems were limited or lost.",
      "Every culture has made music, but very few wrote it down with precise pitches and rhythms. Melodies were learned by listening and copying. Some ancient societies did develop notation. A clay tablet from the city of Ugarit, in modern Syria, dating to around 1400 BCE, contains instructions for a hymn, and the ancient Greeks used letter symbols for pitch. But these systems were limited or lost. In medieval Europe, the church's huge repertoire of chant, now often called Gregorian chant, was learned by heart. Training a singer to know it all could take many years."],
    ["Neumes and the staff", "Neumes only reminded singers of a melody's shape, until lines showing exact pitch, promoted by Guido of Arezzo, let anyone read an unheard tune.",
      "From around the ninth century, scribes added little marks called neumes above the Latin words in chant books. They showed whether the melody rose or fell, but not exactly how high or how long. They were memory aids: a singer who had never heard a chant could not learn it from neumes alone. Gradually, scribes placed them at different heights, so position hinted at pitch.\n\nAround 1030, the Italian monk Guido of Arezzo championed notes on horizontal lines, each representing a fixed pitch, with letters that became clefs. Singers could now read a melody they had never heard, and Guido claimed young singers could learn in a short time what once took years. Music could travel on parchment alone."],
    ["The origin of solfège", "The syllables used to sing scales come from the first syllables of lines in a Latin hymn that Guido used as a teaching tool.",
      "Guido also gave singers a memory trick. He used a Latin hymn to Saint John the Baptist in which each of the first six lines began one step higher than the last. The first syllables of those lines were ut, re, mi, fa, sol and la. By learning the hymn, students fixed the sound of each step in their minds.\n\nOver the centuries, ut was replaced in most countries by do, which is easier to sing. A seventh syllable, si, was added later, and in the nineteenth century English teachers changed it to ti so every syllable began with a different letter. This system, called solfège, is still used to teach singing around the world."],
    ["Writing down time", "As composers wrote for several voices at once, note shapes were invented to show how long each note lasts.",
      "Showing pitch was only half the problem. As composers wrote for several voices singing different lines at once, called polyphony, singers needed to know exactly how long each note lasted so they stayed together. In the thirteenth century, theorists such as Franco of Cologne set out rules in which the shape of a note indicated its duration. This system, called mensural notation, developed over the following centuries. Notes gradually changed from black squares and diamonds to the round open and filled heads used today. Bar lines became common only in the seventeenth century. Time signatures, rests and ties followed."],
    ["Print and other systems", "Printed music from 1501 spread compositions across Europe, and notation is only one way to record music.",
      "Copying music by hand was slow and expensive. In 1501, the Venetian printer Ottaviano Petrucci published the Odhecaton, generally regarded as the first book of polyphonic music printed with movable type. Printed music spread compositions across Europe and created a market of amateurs who bought music to play at home.\n\nStaff notation is not universal. Renaissance lute players used tablature, which shows where to put the fingers rather than which notes sound, as modern guitar tabs do. India, China, Indonesia and other traditions have their own systems, and many jazz, folk and popular musicians learn by ear. Notation struggles to capture subtle timing, tone colour and improvisation. In the twentieth century, some composers used graphic scores of drawings and instructions."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How was the huge repertoire of medieval church chant mostly learned?", ["By heart, which took singers many years","From printed books given to every singer","From detailed staff notation on parchment","By copying a single teacher's written lessons"], 0, "Without precise notation, music depended on memory and teachers, so training took years."],
    [1, "What did Guido of Arezzo's staff make possible?", ["Printing music with movable type","Recording exactly how long each note lasts","Reading a melody you had never heard before","Singing in several voices at once"], 2, "Lines representing fixed pitches meant a singer no longer needed a teacher to learn each chant."],
    [2, "Where do the syllables ut, re, mi, fa, sol and la come from?", ["The names of six medieval musical instruments","The first syllables of lines in a Latin hymn","The first six letters of the Greek alphabet","The names of six saints in a medieval calendar"], 1, "Each line of the hymn began one step higher, so learning it fixed the sound of each step."],
    [3, "Why did composers need ways to notate rhythm?", ["To help singers hide mistakes during performance","To let audiences read the music while listening","To replace the staff with a simpler system","To keep voices singing different lines together"], 3, "Polyphony only works if every singer knows how long each note lasts, so note shapes showed duration."],
    [4, "What does guitar tablature show?", ["Where to put the fingers on the strings","The sounding notes, written on a staff","Only the length of each chord in a piece","How loudly to pluck each string in turn"], 0, "Tablature shows finger positions rather than which notes sound, as Renaissance lute notation did."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Where do do, re and mi come from? A medieval monk had a clever memory trick."});

WB.L("vermeer-light", "arts", "advanced", "Vermeer: the mystery of light",
  "Barely three dozen paintings, almost no letters, no surviving drawings. Johannes Vermeer painted quiet rooms full of light, and art detectives are still arguing about how he did it.",
  [
    ["Few paintings, fewer records", "Vermeer left only about 34 to 37 paintings and almost no personal records, so much about him is guesswork.",
      "Johannes Vermeer was baptised in Delft, in the Dutch Republic, in 1632. His father was a weaver who also dealt in art, and Johannes took over the business. He married Catharina Bolnes, from a Catholic family, in 1653, and they had a large family. He joined the painters' guild and later served as one of its heads.\n\nYet he left no diaries, no letters about his art and no drawing that can confidently be attributed to him. Nearly everything known comes from legal and financial documents. Scholars attribute only about 34 to 37 surviving paintings to him, and some attributions are disputed. He seems to have worked very slowly, perhaps finishing only a few a year."],
    ["Light from the left", "Vermeer's quiet interiors are famous for the subtle way they capture daylight from a window on the left.",
      "A striking number of Vermeer's paintings show a quiet room lit by a window on the left. The figures are usually one or two women, reading letters, pouring milk, playing music or weighing pearls. A local collector, Pieter van Ruijven, appears to have bought many of them, which may have let Vermeer work carefully.\n\nHe watched how daylight behaves. In The Milkmaid, the whitewashed wall is full of subtle shifts, tiny nail holes and marks. Shadows are not just darker; they pick up colour from nearby surfaces. Tiny dots of light paint suggest sparkle on bread, metal or fabric. They resemble the blurred highlights of an out-of-focus lens, which has fuelled debate about his methods."],
    ["Expensive blue", "Vermeer used costly ultramarine freely, even in shadows, which is puzzling for an artist who died in debt.",
      "Natural ultramarine was made from lapis lazuli, a semi-precious stone mined in what is now Afghanistan. It cost more than many other pigments, and many painters saved it for important details, such as the robes of the Virgin Mary.\n\nVermeer used it remarkably freely. It appears in the blue and yellow headscarf of the Girl with a Pearl Earring and in the apron of The Milkmaid. Technical studies have also found ultramarine mixed into shadows and underlayers, where it helps create cool, luminous tones. Such lavish spending is puzzling for an artist who ended his life in debt. It remains part of the mystery of his methods and his finances."],
    ["The camera obscura debate", "Some argue Vermeer used optical aids, but there is no documentary proof, and genius still shaped every choice.",
      "A camera obscura is a darkened box or room in which a lens projects an image of the outside scene onto a surface. The device was known in Vermeer's day. Delft was home to the microscope pioneer Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, later trustee of Vermeer's bankrupt estate, though nothing suggests they discussed optics.\n\nIn 2001 the historian Philip Steadman argued that the geometry of several Vermeer rooms fits a camera obscura, and David Hockney suggested Old Masters used optical aids. In the 2013 documentary Tim's Vermeer, inventor Tim Jenison used a lens and mirror to produce a strikingly close copy of The Music Lesson without painting training. Critics reply that possible is not proven. No document mentions Vermeer owning such a device, and composition, colour and mood still demanded his skill."],
    ["Lost and found", "Vermeer died in debt, was largely forgotten for two centuries, and was revived by a French critic.",
      "In 1672 France invaded the Dutch Republic and the art market collapsed. Vermeer's income dried up. He died in December 1675, aged 43, leaving his family deeply in debt. His widow later said the stress of financial ruin had contributed to his death.\n\nOver the following centuries his name faded, and some paintings were sold under other artists' names. In the 1860s the French critic Théophile Thoré-Bürger published enthusiastic articles about him and helped restore his reputation. Since then his fame has soared. The Girl with a Pearl Earring is now one of the best-known paintings in the world, and a major Vermeer exhibition in Amsterdam in 2023 sold out completely."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes Vermeer unusual for a famous painter?", ["He left few paintings and almost no personal records","He signed every work with a detailed diary entry","He painted hundreds of works for a busy market","He kept careful letters explaining his methods"], 0, "Only about 34 to 37 paintings survive and almost no personal records, so much about him is inferred."],
    [1, "What detail shows how closely Vermeer observed daylight?", ["Plain white walls with no variation at all","Shadows that pick up colour from nearby surfaces","Light that always comes from the right","Figures lit by candles rather than windows"], 1, "His shadows and whitewashed walls hold subtle colour shifts, showing close study of how daylight behaves."],
    [2, "Why is Vermeer's lavish use of ultramarine puzzling?", ["The pigment was cheap and easy to buy in Delft","He used it only for the robes of the Virgin Mary","It was costly, yet he used it freely despite debts","Studies found he never used it in shadows at all"], 2, "Ultramarine was expensive, so his generous use sits oddly with his money troubles."],
    [3, "What is the fair conclusion about Vermeer and the camera obscura?", ["A surviving receipt proves he bought one in Delft","It is possible but unproven, and skill still mattered","Experts agree he never used any optical aid at all","Leeuwenhoek personally taught him how to use one"], 1, "Showing that a method is possible does not prove he used it, and no document mentions such a device."],
    [4, "Why did Vermeer's reputation revive in the 1860s?", ["His paintings were found under other names in Delft","A French critic wrote enthusiastically about him","The Dutch art market recovered after the invasion","His widow published his private letters"], 1, "After two centuries of fading, critic Thoré-Bürger's articles helped restore his reputation."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Barely three dozen paintings survive, and nobody agrees how he made them."});

